```ts
import { Component, ChangeDetectionStrategy, input, computed } from '@angular/core';
import { Order } from './order.model';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-order-summary',
  standalone: true,
  changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush,
  template: `
    <h2>{{ order().id }}</h2>
    <p>Total: {{ formattedTotal() }}</p>
  `,
})
export class OrderSummaryComponent {
  readonly order = input.required<Order>();

  readonly formattedTotal = computed(() => this.order().total.toFixed(2));
}
```

Key points:

- `input()` returns an `InputSignal<T>` (writable via `input()`, read-only via `input.required<T>()` when there is no default). `input.required<Order>()` is the right choice if the parent must always pass `order`; otherwise `input<Order>()` gives a possibly-`undefined` value.
- Read it by calling it: `this.order()` in the class, `order()` in the template. It is a function/signal, not a plain field — do not write `this.order` or `order` bare.
- Derive anything from it with `computed()`, as above. Do not mutate other state or call HTTP inside `computed()`.
- Do not redeclare a plain `@Input() order: Order` field alongside it — mixing styles in one component is the thing to avoid. Also don't shadow with a getter; keep `order` as the signal and read `this.order()`.
- Pair it with `ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush`; reading `order()` in the template registers the dependency automatically, so an OnPush component updates when the parent changes the input, no extra wiring needed.
- If the parent needs two-way binding (the child also emits an update), use `model<Order>()` instead, which is the writable-signal counterpart.

For an output alongside it, use `output<Order>()` (or `output<void>()`) rather than `@Output()` + `EventEmitter`, again for consistency with the codebase's signal style.

